menu "BlueRetro"

    config BLUERETRO_HW2
        bool "Enable support for HW2 features"
        default y
        help
            Select this to enable HW2 features like port detection.

    config BLUERETRO_PWR_STATE_RESTORE
        bool "Restore console power state after a power loss"
        depends on BLUERETRO_HW2
        default y
        help
            Mirror the console power state to the filesystem and re-assert it
            early in boot. Needed when the stock bistable relay is replaced by
            a volatile latch, such as a flip-flop driving a MOSFET switch,
            which comes up off whenever the adapter loses power. The stock
            relay held its own state and did not need this. An adapter with
            no stored state yet comes up on.

    config BLUERETRO_IO0_QDP_PWR_OFF
        bool "Power the console off on an IO0 quick double press"
        depends on BLUERETRO_HW2
        default n
        help
            Upstream behaviour since v24.04: a short IO0 press resets the
            console, and a second press within two seconds powers it off.
            The window is wide and the gesture is easy to hit by accident
            when you only meant to reset, so it is off by default here.
            Reset, pairing and factory reset on IO0 are unaffected.

    config BLUERETRO_IO0_RST_HOLD
        bool "Hold the console in reset on an IO0 triple press and hold"
        depends on BLUERETRO_HW2 && !BLUERETRO_IO0_QDP_PWR_OFF
        default y
        help
            Tap IO0 three times in quick succession and keep the third tap
            held down: the console reset line stays asserted until you let
            go. GameBoy Interface parks the GameBoy Player module while reset
            is held, which is what allows swapping GameBoy carts warm.

            The first two taps reset the console as they normally would. A
            third tap that is not actually held still acts as a plain reset.

            Mutually exclusive with the quick double press power off, which
            claims the same chained tap timing.

    config BLUERETRO_GC_OTA
        bool "Accept firmware updates over the GameCube controller port"
        default n
        help
            Adds two vendor SI opcodes, 0x1E and 0x1F, that let GameCube
            homebrew push a firmware image into the OTA partition without
            Bluetooth. Useful as a recovery path when the Bluetooth stack
            itself is what got broken.

            Data arrives eight bytes at a time and is staged in RAM, then
            flushed a kilobyte at a time; the status opcode reports busy while
            flash is being written so the console knows to wait. Prototype.

    config BLUERETRO_GC_PAIR
        bool "See and forget pairings from the GameCube port"
        default n
        help
            Adds vendor SI opcodes 0x2F to 0x31, which list what the
            adapter has paired with and remove entries from it.

            Device slots turn straight into console ports, so anything
            that once paired and is not a controller keeps taking port
            one from something that should have it. Forgetting it is the
            answer that stays answered. Prototype.

    config BLUERETRO_GC_CFG
        bool "Back up and restore settings over the GameCube port"
        default n
        help
            Adds vendor SI opcodes 0x2B to 0x2E, which copy the config
            blob out to the card and back again.

            Restoring an adapter that lost its filesystem is the obvious
            use; provisioning a batch of them identically from one card,
            without driving a browser once per unit, is the one that
            earns it. Costs a staging buffer the size of the config.
            Prototype.

    config BLUERETRO_GC_BOOT
        bool "Pick the firmware slot from the GameCube port"
        default n
        help
            Adds vendor SI opcodes 0x27 to 0x29, which report what is
            installed in the factory and both OTA slots and let one be
            selected to boot next.

            The updater alternates between the two OTA slots, so the
            firmware from before the last update is still intact, and the
            factory image behind it has never been written. This turns a
            bad update into a menu entry. Prototype.

    config BLUERETRO_GC_LOG
        bool "Download the Bluetooth debug log over the GameCube port"
        default n
        help
            Adds vendor SI opcodes 0x24 to 0x26, which turn the debug capture
            bank on and off, report how much has been captured, and read it
            back a chunk at a time.

            The capture itself already exists and already lands in the memory
            card buffer. What was missing was any way to reach it without the
            Bluetooth web config, which is no help when Bluetooth is the thing
            being diagnosed. Prototype.

    config BLUERETRO_GC_APP
        bool "Expose adapter state to the GameCube companion app"
        default n
        help
            Adds vendor SI opcode 0x21, which reports the raw controller state
            as it arrives over Bluetooth, before any mapping is applied. Lets
            GameCube homebrew show a live input viewer, and is the groundwork
            for an on screen mapping wizard.

            Costs a small snapshot on every controller report. Prototype.

    config BLUERETRO_CTRL_MAP
        bool "Remember mappings per controller rather than per port"
        default n
        help
            Mappings normally belong to a console port, so changing which
            controller connects first hands one pad another's layout.

            With this on, a mapping made with the GameCube app is also saved
            against that controller's Bluetooth address and reloaded whenever
            it connects, on whichever port it lands. Stored as small files
            beside the config rather than inside it, so the config layout that
            the web tool and the config version machinery depend on is left
            alone.

            A controller that has never been mapped keeps whatever the port
            already had. One using a rotating private address will not be
            recognised again once it changes.

    config BLUERETRO_GC_FACE_MAP_TOGGLE
        bool "Let PAD_MS flip the GameCube face buttons between layouts"
        default n
        help
            Upstream behaviour: pressing and releasing PAD_MS swaps the face
            buttons between position based and name based mapping, so a
            Nintendo layout pad can be made to match an Xbox one.

            It is a bare button press, not a combo, with nothing on screen to
            say which layout is active. Hitting it by accident leaves A dead
            and X sending A, which is a long way to walk back from. Off by
            default here, since a Switch 2 GameCube pad is already laid out
            like the console it is plugged into.

    choice
        prompt "Select universal or specific system"
        default BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_UNIVERSAL
        help
            BlueRetro is an universal adapter but you may force it to support
            only one system.

        config BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_UNIVERSAL
            bool "UNIVERSAL"
            help
                Select this to support all systems.

        config BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_PARALLEL_1P
            bool "PARALLEL_1P_PP"
            help
                Select this to support only PARALLEL_1P push-pull.

        config BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_PARALLEL_2P
            bool "PARALLEL_2P_PP"
            help
                Select this to support only PARALLEL_2P push-pull.

        config BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_NES
            bool "NES"
            help
                Select this to support only NES.

        config BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_PCE
            bool "PCE"
            help
                Select this to support only PCE.

        config BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_GENESIS
            bool "MEGADRIVE/GENESIS"
            help
                Select this to support only MEGADRIVE/GENESIS.

        config BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_SNES
            bool "SNES"
            help
                Select this to support only SNES.

        config BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_CDI
            bool "CD-i"
            help
                Select this to support only CD-i.

        config BLUERETRO_SYSTEM_CD32
